WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.966
Larissa put a research agent into production a few weeks ago.

00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:12.196
It takes a market question, searches the web, reads sources, and writes a report.

00:00:12.496 --> 00:00:14.006
The first weeks went fine.

00:00:14.306 --> 00:00:16.658
Then three kinds of complaints started.

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:18.754
Some tasks never ended.

00:00:19.054 --> 00:00:22.969
The agent kept looking for one more source, until the budget ran out.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:25.387
Others ended too soon.

00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:30.801
After two searches, the agent wrote a confident report with shallow information.

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:33.719
And some ended with: I couldn't finish.

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:37.039
Without saying what it had found, or why it stopped.

00:00:37.689 --> 00:00:39.313
All three share one root.

00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:42.636
Done was defined in one sentence of the prompt.

00:00:42.936 --> 00:00:45.923
When you have enough information, write the report.

00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:51.237
The model decided alone what enough meant, and there was no other way to end.

00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:56.672
In this lesson, you'll learn to design the end of an agent, so every run finishes,

00:00:56.672 --> 00:00:58.677
and finishes in the right state.

00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:02.465
Treat the end as part of the design.

00:01:02.765 --> 00:01:07.544
With four explicit exits, each one checked by code whenever possible.

00:01:08.194 --> 00:01:09.117
Success.

00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:13.235
The goal was met, and there's a verifiable criterion for it.

00:01:13.535 --> 00:01:14.807
The test passes.

00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:18.557
The report has the required number of valid sources.

00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:19.625
Limit.

00:01:19.925 --> 00:01:22.557
The step, time or cost budget ran out.

00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:26.442
The agent delivers what it has, and says it stopped at the limit.

00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:27.832
Giving up.

00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:34.304
The agent concluded it can't do the task, for lack of access or information.

00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:35.811
And it explains why.

00:01:36.461 --> 00:01:37.815
And asking for help.

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:42.721
The agent needs a decision, or a fact, that only a person has.

00:01:43.371 --> 00:01:48.642
The model can signal success, giving up, or asking for help, through a tool of its

00:01:48.642 --> 00:01:50.827
own, or through structured output.

00:01:51.127 --> 00:01:53.693
Your code confirms success when it can.

00:01:53.993 --> 00:01:56.586
And the limit always belongs to your code.

00:01:56.886 --> 00:02:01.116
A referee ends the match at the whistle, not when one team gets tired.

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:05.485
Each turn of the loop ends with a decision from the model.

00:02:06.135 --> 00:02:08.966
But code checks the budget before anything else.

00:02:09.266 --> 00:02:13.950
With no budget left, the run stops with its current state, and the reason.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.282
The model signals one of four intents, in a structured way.

00:02:18.932 --> 00:02:20.409
And done isn't enough.

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:24.293
Code checks the success criterion, when it's verifiable.

00:02:24.943 --> 00:02:27.386
Confirmed success ends the task.

00:02:28.036 --> 00:02:32.478
Giving up is a valid exit, with an explanation of what was tried.

00:02:33.128 --> 00:02:36.927
And asking for help pauses the run, until a person answers.

00:02:38.127 --> 00:02:39.731
Back to Larissa's agent.

00:02:40.031 --> 00:02:43.250
The success criterion became a rule in code.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:48.857
The report must cite at least three independent sources, and every link must open.

00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:51.074
The budget is twenty-five steps.

00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:54.662
At step six, the model says: report ready.

00:02:54.962 --> 00:02:57.750
The check finds two valid sources out of three.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:58.977
Rejected.

00:02:59.277 --> 00:03:01.733
And the model gets the reason back.

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:04.099
One independent source is missing.

00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:06.221
So it keeps going.

00:03:06.521 --> 00:03:09.202
At step fourteen, it says done again.

00:03:09.502 --> 00:03:12.761
Four valid sources, all links open.

00:03:13.061 --> 00:03:13.804
Accepted.

00:03:14.104 --> 00:03:16.084
Stop reason: success.

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:20.411
That first done was exactly the shallow report from the complaints.

00:03:20.711 --> 00:03:24.165
The check returned the reason, and the model kept working.

00:03:24.465 --> 00:03:28.499
Without it, the task would have stopped at step six, one source short.

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:33.862
Now a question with no public answer, like a private company's profit margin.

00:03:34.162 --> 00:03:35.941
There are two good exits.

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.762
At step nine, the agent gives up.

00:03:39.062 --> 00:03:41.475
No public source gives the margin.

00:03:41.775 --> 00:03:45.224
I only found third party estimates, listed below.

00:03:45.874 --> 00:03:51.780
Or, if it keeps trying, code stops it at step twenty-five, and delivers a summary of

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.764
what it found.

00:03:53.414 --> 00:03:59.024
The bad exit, the one this pattern prevents, is a confident report with a made up

00:03:59.024 --> 00:03:59.509
number.

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:01.831
Here's one for you.

00:04:02.131 --> 00:04:06.901
Your coding agent calls finish, with done, and the note: fixed the bug.

00:04:07.201 --> 00:04:08.681
The tests are still red.

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:10.324
What should the loop do?

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:18.178
Reject the success, tell the model which tests fail, and keep looping.

00:04:18.478 --> 00:04:22.756
The model's declaration is a request to end, not proof.

00:04:23.056 --> 00:04:26.538
When there's a verifiable criterion, code decides.

00:04:27.188 --> 00:04:28.911
And it's not a give-up either.

00:04:29.211 --> 00:04:30.607
The model didn't give up.

00:04:30.907 --> 00:04:32.181
It thought it was done.

00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:36.883
Ending here would waste a task that might be two steps from passing.

00:04:37.183 --> 00:04:41.431
And the budget still applies, so the loop can't get stuck on this forever.

00:04:42.631 --> 00:04:44.155
Don't guess the ceiling.

00:04:44.455 --> 00:04:49.986
Look at the traces of runs that ended in success, and see how many steps, seconds

00:04:49.986 --> 00:04:51.489
and dollars they used.

00:04:51.789 --> 00:04:56.262
Then put the ceiling above the vast majority of them, with room to spare.

00:04:56.912 --> 00:05:02.524
Say ninety-five percent of successful runs use up to fourteen steps, and the longest

00:05:02.524 --> 00:05:03.460
uses nineteen.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.988
A ceiling of twenty-five leaves room for the hard case, and cuts a stuck loop early.

00:05:09.288 --> 00:05:14.695
A ceiling of two hundred, just in case, lets a stuck run spend ten times more before

00:05:14.695 --> 00:05:15.275
it stops.

00:05:15.925 --> 00:05:18.980
And revisit the limits when the task type changes.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.472
A ceiling that's right for fix one test is too short for migrate a module.

00:05:24.672 --> 00:05:26.553
Now the common mistakes.

00:05:26.853 --> 00:05:28.888
A limit that throws an exception.

00:05:29.188 --> 00:05:33.603
The task vanishes, with no state, no record and no answer.

00:05:33.903 --> 00:05:36.432
The limit is an exit like the others.

00:05:36.732 --> 00:05:39.946
It delivers what was done, and says it stopped there.

00:05:40.596 --> 00:05:43.209
Success means the model said it was done.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:47.050
If there's a test, a schema or a count, use it.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.878
The model's opinion only decides when there's nothing to check.

00:05:51.528 --> 00:05:53.824
A prompt that pushes toward giving up.

00:05:54.124 --> 00:05:58.241
Phrases like when in doubt, give up, make the give-up rate spike.

00:05:58.541 --> 00:06:00.876
Say exactly when to give up.

00:06:01.176 --> 00:06:05.584
Missing access, data that doesn't exist, permission denied.

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:09.460
And asking for help with nobody on the other end.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.700
If the person takes hours, the run hangs.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.278
Set a waiting deadline, and decide what happens when it expires.

00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:24.086
In production, record a stop reason on every trace, and look at the distribution per

00:06:24.086 --> 00:06:24.754
task type.

00:06:25.054 --> 00:06:27.846
Success, limit, give-up and help.

00:06:28.496 --> 00:06:30.557
And watch the limit stops closely.

00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:36.648
Say in the browser agent, limit stops go from three percent to twenty-five percent

00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:38.132
of runs, in one week.

00:06:38.432 --> 00:06:40.034
Nobody deployed anything.

00:06:40.334 --> 00:06:43.526
Doubling the budget only lets stuck runs spend more.

00:06:43.826 --> 00:06:47.578
Switching models is the most expensive and least likely guess.

00:06:47.878 --> 00:06:49.089
Open the traces.

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:53.876
A jump like that, with no code change, usually comes from the outside world.

00:06:54.176 --> 00:06:57.589
A portal changed, or a tool started failing silently.

00:06:57.889 --> 00:07:01.499
That's exactly why the stop reason goes on the trace.

00:07:02.149 --> 00:07:05.596
And build at least one eval case per exit.

00:07:05.896 --> 00:07:08.284
An easy task expects success.

00:07:08.584 --> 00:07:13.955
A task that's impossible on purpose, like a portal that's offline, expects a

00:07:13.955 --> 00:07:14.520
give-up.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.916
A task that depends on a business decision expects a help request.

00:07:20.216 --> 00:07:25.511
And a tool that answers okay with no progress expects a limit stop.

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:30.999
If the agent answers the impossible task with success, you found the most dangerous

00:07:30.999 --> 00:07:31.749
case of all.

00:07:32.049 --> 00:07:33.388
A false success.

00:07:34.588 --> 00:07:39.856
On the page, every version gives the model a finish tool, with three kinds, done,

00:07:39.856 --> 00:07:44.409
give up and need help, and runs the loop under a step and time budget.

00:07:45.059 --> 00:07:48.049
When the signal is done, code runs the tests.

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:50.302
If they pass, success.

00:07:50.602 --> 00:07:54.056
If not, the model gets told, and the loop goes on.

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:59.084
And when the budget runs out, it returns a limit stop, with a summary.

00:07:59.734 --> 00:08:04.430
In Python and TypeScript, the signal is read inside the loop.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:08.266
In LangGraph, the same signal is read on an edge.

00:08:08.566 --> 00:08:13.867
Limit, success, give-up and human become destinations in the graph.

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:16.073
Not sentences in a prompt.

00:08:17.273 --> 00:08:19.532
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:23.459
An agent needs a designed ending, not a hopeful one.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:30.025
Success that code can confirm, a limit that code owns, a give-up that explains, and

00:08:30.025 --> 00:08:31.685
a way to ask a person.

00:08:31.985 --> 00:08:37.245
Then every run ends in a known state, and the user knows what they got, and why.

00:08:37.895 --> 00:08:43.547
In the next lesson, we switch from how an agent ends to what it holds in its hands:

00:08:43.547 --> 00:08:44.364
tool sprawl.

00:08:44.664 --> 00:08:45.435
See you there.
